# 7. SMS Management Module

This is one of the most important modules.

Implement a complete SMS bridge using the Android Telephony framework.

## 7.1 Incoming SMS

Implement:

SmsReceiver.kt

Support receiving new SMS messages using the documented Android broadcast APIs.

Handle:

- Multipart SMS.
- Different SIM subscriptions.
- Sender number.
- Message body.
- Received timestamp.
- Subscription ID.
- Encoding differences.
- Duplicate broadcast prevention.

Use SMS_RECEIVED_ACTION for supported companion-mode operation.

When the application is the designated default SMS handler, also implement the appropriate SMS_DELIVER_ACTION workflow and persist incoming messages to the system SMS Provider as required.

Never persist the same SMS twice.

Do not assume SMS_DELIVER_ACTION is delivered to non-default SMS applications.

## 7.2 SMS History

Implement:

SmsProviderDataSource.kt

Read existing SMS records using ContentResolver and Telephony.Sms.

Required data:

- Provider message ID.
- Thread ID.
- Sender / recipient.
- Message body.
- Received date.
- Sent date.
- Message type.
- Read state.
- SIM subscription.
- Delivery status where available.

Support:

- Inbox.
- Sent.
- Failed.
- Draft.
- Read / Unread.

Do not assume SMS Provider records contain every advanced carrier feature.

MMS and RCS are outside the initial scope.

## 7.3 SMS Sending

Implement:

SmsSender.kt

Provide:

suspend fun sendSms(
    destination: String,
    message: String,
    subscriptionId: Int?
): SmsSendResult

Use SmsManager.

Handle:

- Runtime permissions.
- Invalid phone number.
- Missing SIM.
- Multiple SIM cards.
- Message segmentation.
- Multipart messages.
- SMS sending failures.
- Carrier restrictions.
- Sent PendingIntents.
- Delivery PendingIntents.
- Service unavailable.
- Device without telephony messaging capability.

The selected SIM must be explicit where multiple subscriptions exist.

Do not silently select an arbitrary SIM.

Provide proper sending states:

QUEUED
SENDING
SENT
FAILED
DELIVERED
DELIVERY_UNKNOWN

Only mark delivery as confirmed when a valid delivery report is received.

Use idempotency keys for outgoing requests.

If an iPhone command is repeated, do not send the same SMS again.

Maintain a persistent outbox for operations awaiting completion.

## 7.4 SMS Role and Permission Management

Implement clear permission and role handling.

Potential permissions:

READ_SMS
RECEIVE_SMS
SEND_SMS
READ_PHONE_STATE

Request other SMS-related permissions only when the selected implementation genuinely needs them.

Support two operating modes:

A. Companion Mode

- Uses granted permissions where available.
- Receives eligible SMS broadcasts.
- Reads accessible historical SMS records.
- Does not pretend to be the default SMS application.
- Exposes reduced functionality when permissions are unavailable.

B. Default SMS Mode

- Requests the Android SMS role only after explaining its implications.
- Implements the required default SMS handling flow.
- Handles incoming SMS delivery through the correct broadcast.
- Uses the system SMS Provider appropriately.
- Provides a functional Android SMS-management fallback if needed.

If Google Play distribution is intended, comply with the active SMS and Call Log permissions policy.

Do not assume that a sideloaded application is automatically approved for Google Play distribution.

The permission screen must clearly explain why access is required.

